; THE UNSIGNED WORD ON GO — ADR 0026 and ADR 0033, chosen by ir/words.go.
;
; THE ALGEBRA. Three sets of integers and one ring:
;
;   S = [−2^63, 2^63)   the signed word, Go's `int`;
;   U = [0, 2^64)       the unsigned word, Go's `uint64` (go.oro's `u64`);
;   r : ℤ → ℤ/2^64      the residue map, a surjective ring homomorphism.
;
; r restricted to S and r restricted to U are each a bijection onto ℤ/2^64, so
; both are sets of representatives, and a 64-bit machine operation computes
; r of ℤ's result on either. Write r_U and r_S for the representative in U and
; in S. On S ∩ U = [0, 2^63) both are the identity.
;
; THE NAMES, as the functions on ℤ they compute:
;
;   u64+ u64- u64*    (a, b) ↦ r_U(a ∘ b), ℤ/2^64's ring operations on their
;                     representatives in U: total on U × U, and equal to ℤ's
;                     a ∘ b iff a ∘ b ∈ U. Where ir/words.go writes one, it is
;                     a COUNTED operation: ir.Decide proves the result inside U
;                     or refuses the program (decide.go), so there it IS ℤ's.
;                     A program may also name one, `(go.u64+ a b)`, and then it
;                     gets the modular operation it named: uncounted, with the
;                     set U as its result's fact, which is all the declaration
;                     claims (TestAProgramsU64SumIsTheHostsModularSum). That is
;                     a target-native name, and the language's ℤ/2^n is not
;                     built (the wall hash/fnv will hit).
;   u64/ u64%         ℤ's truncating division on U. For a, b ∈ U and b ≠ 0 the
;                     quotient and remainder are in U and neither wraps, so the
;                     only precondition is the divisor's, declared below. Order
;                     and division do NOT commute with r, which is why these
;                     and the comparisons are selected only where both operands
;                     are proven in U.
;   u64< … u64=       ℤ's order and equality on U, which is Go's unsigned
;                     comparison.
;   u64-of            r_U on S, Go's `uint64(x)`: the identity on [0, 2^63),
;                     x + 2^64 below it.
;   int-of-u64        r_S on U, Go's `int(x)`: the identity on [0, 2^63),
;                     x − 2^64 above it.
;
; WHY A SELECTED FUNCTION COMPUTES THE LANGUAGE'S INTEGERS (ir/words.go). An
; operand of + − · is converted by r freely, even where that changes its
; integer, because r is a homomorphism and the result is proven in its own
; realization. Every other conversion — an operand of / % or a comparison, a
; value flowing into a declared position — is at a value proven in S ∩ U,
; where it is the identity, or the program is refused naming the interval.
; ir/interval.go transfers each name by its exact image under r
; (irstep4b §3, irstep4c).
(target go
  (module go
    (sig u64+  ((a u64) (b u64)) u64 pure (host expr "(%s + %s)"))
    (sig u64-  ((a u64) (b u64)) u64 pure (host expr "(%s - %s)"))
    (sig u64*  ((a u64) (b u64)) u64 pure (host expr "(%s * %s)"))
    (sig u64/  ((a u64) (b u64)) u64 pure (where (!= b 0)) (host expr "(%s / %s)"))
    (sig u64%  ((a u64) (b u64)) u64 pure (where (!= b 0)) (host expr "(%s %% %s)"))
    (sig u64<  ((a u64) (b u64)) bool pure (host expr "(%s < %s)"))
    (sig u64<= ((a u64) (b u64)) bool pure (host expr "(%s <= %s)"))
    (sig u64>  ((a u64) (b u64)) bool pure (host expr "(%s > %s)"))
    (sig u64>= ((a u64) (b u64)) bool pure (host expr "(%s >= %s)"))
    (sig u64=  ((a u64) (b u64)) bool pure (host expr "(%s == %s)"))
    (sig u64-of     ((x int)) u64 pure (host expr "uint64(%s)"))
    (sig int-of-u64 ((x u64)) int pure (host expr "int(%s)"))))
